Ultimate Texas Hold'em is a variant of poker that is basically the same as Texas Hold'em. In this variant you only do not play against other players, but only against the dealer.
In this article, we'll explain how to play Ultimate Texas Hold'Em and what strategies to follow, so you know what to expect when you visit an online casino.
On the table, whether live or online, you will see different betting circles: Ante, Blinds, Trips and Play.
Before you get cards as a player you must first place two bets. The first is known as the 'ante' and the other as a 'blind'.
You place this bet in the 'Play' box and can be three or four times the 'ante'. After this, the rest of the cards are dealt and you cannot place any more bets.
With the 2 cards in your hand and 3 cards on the table, you can now try to make winning combinations as a player. We describe these combinations later in the game rules on this page.
You can also choose to check again. If you already bet on pre-flop, you can't do anything now.
That bet must be an amount equal to 3 or 4 times the ante bet. You can also check, which means you don't place a bet, but you want to see more cards.
Ultimate Texas Hold'em combines the turn and the river in one round. In other words, the dealer deals 2 more cards face up after which the hands are compared.
However, the blind bet is a bit more complicated and, in addition to having a better hand than the dealer, it also depends on the combination of cards you have been able to make. At the payout, a 'straight' is the lowest combination that yields a payout.
You can also place an optional trips bet at the start of each hand. This bet is paid out based on the strength of your hand only, and you don't have to beat the dealer to win.
To play this game well you need a strategy, or 'Know when to Hold'em'. The fact that there are different decision points on each hand quickly complicates things.
It's also easier to come up with a plan that won't allow you to make big mistakes than it seems. With this basic strategy below, you can start a game of Ultimate Texas Hold'em with confidence without fear of losing all your money right away.
The first decision point is during the pre-fold, where you choose whether to raise the first 4x with your first hand. We recommend raising if your hand contains the following cards:
King and a three or higher
Jack and a nine or higher
With these hands there is a good chance that you can make a winning combination.
If you checked before the flop you still have a chance to place a bet for 2x the ante. You do this in the following situations:
If you can make two pairs or better
If you don't have such a strong hand, try to count how many cards from the dealer could beat you. If the total number of possible cards is 20 or less, you can still bet now.
Free online Ultimate Texas Hold'em games are the perfect way to master the game. You can sharpen your skills and get used to the different types of bets without risking losing money.
In that case, always increase by 4x instead of 3x, unless your balance is low and you can't afford it. All the leading Ultimate Texas Hold'em experts agree that there is no point in raising 3x and that you are losing a lot of money if you raise 3x instead of 4x in pre-flop.
