The only difference is that the poker variants all have their own name as Texas Hold'em or 5 Card Stud, but all versions of mahjong are called mahjong. And that doesn't make it any easier to figure out how the game being played.
All players receive 34 stones in stacks of two with which they have to build a 'wall'.
Each player in turn takes an extra stone from his own wall. If the stone can be used to make a combination, the player keeps the stone.
When a player with the last tile he took has complete sets, he shouts "Mahjong!"
The goal of mahjong is to use the tiles you have to make winning combinations that yield the most points as quickly as possible. The combinations you can make to win are just about endless, which is why mahjong is also called the Game of the Ten Thousand Possibilities or the Game of a Thousand Wonders.
Mahjong is played with a set of tiles ranging from 136 to 144, depending on the version, and a pair of dice. On the stones you see different Chinese symbols.
The goal of mahjong is to create a winning hand with 14 tiles. A winning hand consists of sets of two, three or four tiles, which give a certain number of points.
Purse Three equal stones from one series 3 x bamboo 3
The game ends when a player has made complete sets with the last tile he has taken and has no tile left. The player then shouts “Mahjong!”, Just as one would shout 'Bingo' in a bingo game.
Hidden pungs completed with stones from the wall score higher than visible pungs made with discarded stones. Also, stones with a higher value score more than stones with a lower value.
Extra points can be awarded based on the pair you complete your hand, or your score can be doubled if you win by taking a tile from the wall.
The more moves you think you need, the more defensive you have to play. But sometimes it also pays not to make combinations too early, as it can also lead to stones that don't match at the end.
Because you have to show the combination you want to make when you pick up a discarded stone, you give away information about your hand. Therefore, consider whether you want to provide other players with that information before taking the stone.
There are many different ways to play mahjong. You can only play it as a kind of solitaire, and you can play the game in different variants as a party game or gambling game.
In the Netherlands we also have an official Mahjong association that regularly organizes championships. Each country has its own version of Mahjong, so you can also encounter different versions of the game in an online casino depending on the software used.
Twenty years ago, the Sports Committee of China issued a set of standard rules so that all players around the world can play with the same standardized set of rules. The Chinese version is therefore the most played version and is often played in tournaments.
This version is similar to traditional Chinese mahjong, but the scores are based on a specific pay table with doubles for specific combinations. This variant of Mahjong is quite popular in Asia, especially India.
Practice making three-tile combinations until it is easy for you. Remember you can always add a fourth tile when it becomes available.
Keep in mind that pongs are worth more points than chows. Chows are only valuable if you have four to make a mahjong.
By separating completed groups or setting a few tiles aside, your opponents can easily see what you are working on so they adjust their mahjong strategy accordingly. You can prevent this by keeping your tiles in a straight continuous row.
It's tempting to throw away a stone you can't use, but that's not the most sensible strategy. Instead, you should pay attention to the stones your opponents put away.
