There are three Hall sensors in the stator of the brushless DC motor, and the phases are separated by 120 degrees (see Chapter 2). When their digital output data are combined together, a three-digit number representing the position of the rotor is produced.
Pulse width modulation converts the inverter’s DC voltage into a modulated effective voltage. For example, using a pulse width modulation control signal with a duty cycle of 0% to 100%, a 12-volt battery can be used to apply any voltage from 0 to 12 volts to the motor.
The pulse width modulation switching frequency is an important design factor that must be kept in mind during the power development stage. Increasing the switching frequency will increase the switching loss, but can improve the current stability in a low-inductance motor.
