A coating for solar power generation that can replace silicon News
Using solar panels to absorb the sun’s rays, and then through the photovolt effect, the radiation of the sun’s rays can be converted into electrical energy – this is commonly known as solar power generation, which refers to the solar panels of the main material is “silicon”.It is only because of the high cost of using silicon that solar power has not become a widely used form of electricity generation.
One of the leading research institutions in the field of Solar power is the MIB-Solar Institute at the University of Milan Bicocca, Italy, which is currently experimenting with a coating for Solar power called DSC Technology.DSC stands for dye-sensitised Solar Cell.
It uses a special printing machine to print nanoscale titanium oxide onto a template, which is then immersed in organic paint for 24 hours. When the coating is fixed on the titanium oxide, the solar cell is made.
The advantage of the coating is not only that it is low cost, but also that it is far more environmentally adaptable than “silicon” panels.It works in bad weather or dark conditions, such as overcast or at dawn or dusk.
