In an overloaded world of information, it is essential to communicate clearly, concisely and effectively. People do not have the time to read e-mails the length of a book, and they do not have the patience to interpret poorly constructed e-mails and where useful information is scattered everywhere.
The first step to write clearly is to choose the appropriate format. Do you need to send an informal email?
Or write a formal letter?
In everything you write, your readers, or recipients, must define your tone as well as the aspects of the content.
Once you know what you are writing and for whom you are writing, you must start writing.
Start with your audienceDo not forget that your readers may not know anything about what you tell them. What do they need to know first?
Try a little empathyFor example, if you are writing a sales email for potential customers, why should they care about your product or sales pitch?
Remember the needs of your audience at all times.
Identify your main theme: If you have difficulty defining the main theme of your message, pretend you have 15 seconds left to explain your location. What do you say ?
Use plain language: Unless writing a scientific article, it is generally better to use simple language, a direct language. Do not use long words just to impress people.
Your document should be as user friendly as possible. Use titles, subtitles, bullets and numbers as much as possible to separate the text.
Here are some examples of commonly used words:
The verb "to send" being a verb of the first group, one will always write in the first person of the singular "I send" with an "e". "Shipment" without "e" is a name ("a shipment") and may be plural: "shipments".
One will always write "I join you" with an "s". "Joint" with a "t" is the conjugation of the third person singular "he joins".
Even if "bumper" is affixed to a feminine name, do not give in to temptation and always write "bumper" without "e".
If in English we write "recommendation" with an "e", in French we always write "recommendation" with an "a".
One never writes "in terms of" without an "s". There are indeed always several "terms" in the use of this expression.
Be careful not to be misled by the word "except" which ends with an "s". One never writes "among" with an "s".
Even affixed to a feminine name, "as agreed" is always invariable and never takes an "e". Maintenance / service
The name "interview" without "t" describes an exchange or a "job interview". The conjugated verb in the third person of the singular "maintains" is used when it comes to doing the action of maintaining something.
For this reason, everything you write must be of a quality acceptable to all readers. Verification
Many people hurry to browse their emails, but that's how you miss mistakes. Follow these instructions to check what you have written:
Read the email aloud: This forces you to go slower, which means that you are more likely to detect errors.
Start at the end of your text: Re-read a sentence from the end to the beginning, it helps you focus on the errors and not the content.
