How to write a relevant report?
A colleague has just sent you an email about a meeting you have in an hour. Email is supposed to contain the key information you need to present as part of an important project.
Have you ever faced a situation similar to this one?
The format, as well as your audience, will define your "writing voice", ie how the tone should be formal or relaxed. For example, if you write an email to a potential customer, should it have the same tone as an email to a friend?
Start by identifying who will read your message. Is it addressed to senior managers, the entire team or a small group working on a specific issue?
Once you know what you are writing and for whom you are writing, you must start writing.
Even if "bumper" is affixed to a feminine name, do not give in to temptation and always write "bumper" without "e". Recommendation / recommendation
They may not notice if you make these mistakes. But do not use this as an excuse: there will usually be people, especially senior executives, who will notice!
Check your headers and footers: People often ignore them to focus only on the text. This is not because the headers are fat and fat that they are free of errors!
Use your finger to follow the text as you read: It's another thing that helps you slow down.
Previous How to write clear and professional emails?
So, how to write a relevant report in this context? Its writing is not an easy task especially if you have to highlight all the mandatory elements in a report.
Respect criteria for writing a report is essential, it allows to be objective and faithful to the events. You must avoid giving personal opinions or appreciating what has been decided.
Avoid using personal terms, in other words, avoid the "I" as well as the "WE", all that suggests the personal involvement of the author. Since you must remain as neutral as possible, do not use adjectives or adverbs.
Highlight the date, the participants and the program that was followed during the meetings. Make sure your information is as reliable as possible by updating it as the seminar progresses.
